---
title: Phalacrocoracidae
---
# Phalacrocoracidae

Phalacrocoracidae is the cormorant family, some forty species of long-bodied
seabirds with a hooked bill. They take eels, flatfish and sprats by descending
from the surface and propelling themselves through the water after them, driven
by the feet, and can stay submerged for more than a minute. The plumage is
deliberately wettable, which reduces buoyancy and makes the descent cheap; the
cost is the spread-wing posture adopted afterwards to dry out. See
[[great-cormorant]].
